# Valgrind suppressions for navi's leak check.
#
# Scope: we run with --show-leak-kinds=definite,indirect and treat only those as
# errors, so OpenSSL's process-global "still reachable" caches and "possibly
# lost" interior pointers are already ignored without suppressions. Entries here
# are for the residual *definite/indirect* leaks that are genuinely not navi's
# to free (one-time library/loader initialization). Keep this list short and
# specific; a navi-owned leak must be fixed, not suppressed.

# One-time dynamic-loader init when the first dlopen'd codec/TLS lib is resolved.
{
  dl-init-once
  Memcheck:Leak
  match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
  ...
  fun:_dl_init
}

# chronos leaks a StreamTransport read future when the transport is closed while a
# `readOnce` is in flight with data still buffered -- exactly the shape of navi's
# chronos h2 multiplexer, whose background reader is parked on `recvSome` (a
# `readOnce`) on a busy connection when the client is closed. This is a chronos
# bug, not navi's to free: a pure-chronos reproducer (a bare reader + a server
# that keeps sending, then `closeWait`) leaks the same read future with zero navi
# frames. The stack below is entirely chronos (nimNewObj -> chronos readOnce ->
# the dispatcher poll loop), so a navi-owned leak -- which carries navi frames --
# does not match and still fails the run. Its indirect children (the read's
# buffer, and the TransportError stack trace raised into navi's feedIn on close)
# are covered by match-leak-kinds: indirect.
{
  chronos-streamtransport-read-future-on-close
  Memcheck:Leak
  match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
  fun:malloc
  fun:_ZN6system9allocImplE*
  fun:allocSharedImpl
  fun:_ZN6system12alignedAllocE*
  fun:_ZN6system13alignedAlloc0E*
  fun:nimNewObj
  fun:_ZN8readOnce8readOnceE*
  ...
}
{
  chronos-read-future-error-stacktrace-on-close
  Memcheck:Leak
  match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
  fun:realloc
  fun:_ZN6system11reallocImplE*
  fun:_ZN6system17reallocSharedImplE*
  fun:_ZN6system14alignedReallocE*
  fun:prepareSeqAddUninit
  fun:_ZN6system6setLenE3varI3seqIN6system15StackTraceEntryEEE*
  fun:_ZN6system18auxWriteStackTraceE*
  fun:raiseExceptionEx
  ...
}

# --track-fds also reports descriptors still open at exit (CoreError:FdNotClosed),
# which catches a leaked navi socket or cert file. The event-loop backends
# (asyncdispatch, chronos) keep their dispatcher's own control descriptors open for
# the whole process, though: the epoll instance, the eventfd/pipe used to wake the
# loop, and chronos's timer/signal fds. navi never opens these directly (its
# sockets show fun:socket / SSL frames), so suppress them by their creating
# syscall. A real leaked socket does not match and still fails the run.
{
  eventloop-epoll-fd
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:epoll_create1
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-epoll-fd-legacy
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:epoll_create
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-wakeup-eventfd
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:eventfd
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-wakeup-pipe
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:pipe2
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-timerfd
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:timerfd_create
  ...
}
{
  eventloop-signalfd
  CoreError:FdNotClosed
  fun:signalfd
  ...
}
